# Line endings are decided HERE, not by whatever core.autocrlf happens to be on
# a given machine.
#
# Why this file exists: this box runs core.autocrlf=true, and the pages captured
# from production arrived with MIXED CRLF/LF endings. Git refuses to normalize a
# file whose blob is already mixed (`git ls-files --eol` reports i/mixed), so a
# one-attribute SVG edit to know-the-facts.html rewrote every line ending and
# showed up as a 304-line diff. A real change hidden inside several hundred lines
# of invisible churn is exactly the kind of diff nobody reviews properly.
#
# eol=lf (not just text=auto) so the WORKING TREE is LF too. Otherwise git keeps
# checking these out as CRLF here and re-converting on every commit, and the
# churn comes straight back the next time a file drifts to mixed.
#
# LF is the right choice rather than CRLF: these are static web assets served by
# a CDN and read by browsers, no Windows-only tooling touches them.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never let the above near binary assets -- a stray CRLF->LF pass corrupts an
# image silently. text=auto already sniffs for binary, but the icons and the
# favicon are the files it would be most expensive to get wrong.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
